"importe ≤ lo vencido": what is "lo vencido"? Probably the entire en_retirada amount if matured, or nothing if not. Since there's at most one pending withdrawal conceptually? Actually the order says "crea una entrada en_retirada". It doesn't explicitly limit to one. C-BON-05 says "A lo sumo hay una retirada pendiente por clave; otra se rechaza o consolida por una regla única todavía PENDIENTE." This is an ambiguity! The contract says at most one pending withdrawal per key, but the consolidation rule is PENDING. So what do we do on a second Retiro while one is pending? Options: reject (restrictive) or consolidate. The order doesn't specify. So AMBIGÜEDAD.

Hmm, but the order's §3.15 says "Retiro exige importe ≤ activo (si no, ErrSaldo) y crea una entrada en_retirada". It doesn't mention the single-withdrawal constraint. The contract §5 (C-BON-05) says at most one pending. This is a genuine ambiguity: (a) reject second Retiro with some error; (b) consolidate. The most restrictive is reject. But which error? Not listed explicitly. Could use ErrSaldo? Or ErrOperacionFase? Hmm. Actually "otra se rechaza o consolida por una regla única todavía PENDIENTE" — this is a pending definition. Per the rules, if the contract has a pending rule, we should report as ambiguity and choose the most restrictive (reject). But what error type? Maybe ErrGarantia? No. Hmm. Actually maybe the "most restrictive" that doesn't alter another rule is to reject the second withdrawal with ErrSaldo (since importe > activo? no). Or maybe we allow multiple en_retirada entries, each with its own inicio_slot, which is actually the more permissive reading but simpler and preserves the meaning of other rules (each withdrawal independently counts R_slots from its own start). Actually, does allowing multiple withdrawals alter another rule? C-BON-05 says "A lo sumo hay una retirada pendiente por clave" — so allowing multiple violates that. I think reject is the restrictive reading. Error type: not specified. We could define `ErrRetiroPendiente` but the order's error list doesn't include it. It says "Errores explícitos, al menos: ..." so adding a new error is allowed. But adding a new error type not in the list might be seen as choosing. Hmm.

