### Ambiguities / underspecifications

1. **Fees for deposits.** Order §3.11: "Los depósitos v0 no tienen tarifa ni cambio: Σ entradas == importe exactamente, si no ErrSaldo." Clear. So Deposito(entradas, clave, importe, firmante) consumes UTXOs whose values sum exactly to importe and credits importe to clave's pending garantía. Wait, but C-BON-02 says "acredita exactamente el mismo valor al registro de P, menos una tarifa explícita si existe". Order says no fee. So credit = importe = sum inputs. Good.

But hold on: is `clave` the credited key and `firmante` the one authorizing? X-06: "Depósito que acredita a una clave distinta de la que firma la aceptación" → ErrAutorizacion. So the deposit's `clave` must equal `firmante`? Let's read: "una operación de depósito consume UTXO transparentes autorizados y acredita exactamente el mismo valor al registro de P, menos una tarifa explícita si existe. P firma la aceptación de la responsabilidad." So P is the credited key and P signs. If clave ≠ firmante → ErrAutorizacion.

But then what does "consumo de UTXO autorizados" mean? The firmante must own each input consumed? §4.2: "«autorizado» significa firmante = dueño de cada entrada consumida, o firmante = clave en operaciones de garantía." So for Deposito, firmante = dueño of each input AND firmante = clave. Hmm, "o firmante = clave en operaciones de garantía" — that's for garantía operations. For a deposit, the UTXO inputs are owned by someone; the depositor must own them (firmante = dueño of each input), and also the credited clave = firmante. So both.

Actually wait, can a deposit consume inputs owned by a different key than the credited key? Normally no: you deposit your own coins. X-06 tests clave ≠ firmante → ErrAutorizacion. So firmante must equal clave (the credited key), and also firmante must own each consumed input. Let me apply both.

2. **AltaSector.** `AltaSector(id, clave, firmante)`. SEC-A only. In PoW from H_dep, in PoST. Wait: contract §4 table says Alta de sector (SEC-A) is allowed "sí, desde H_dep" in PoW and "sí" in PoST. But ORDEN §3.7 says: "Fuera de alcance v0 (rechazo explícito ErrFueraDeAlcanceV0): EvidenceTx en fase PoST (liquidación C-EVP/C-SLA no se modela); altas de sector en fase PoST." So altas de sector in PoST are out of scope → ErrFueraDeAlcanceV0. Good, the order overrides. So AltaSector only in PoW phase, at h ≥ H_dep.

3. **Sector registry.** SEC-A: id → (P, estado, alta_en, activa_en, plazo). Alta at PoW height h: creates sector with estado "alta", activa_en = h + M_sec. It becomes active at h_alta + M_sec (promotion). PruebaSector(id, firmante) must be applied with h_prueba ≤ h_alta + P_sec (else ErrPruebaTardia). A sector is active if its proof was applied in h_prueba ≤ h_alta + P_sec and h_alta + M_sec ≤ h current (or any point of PoST if already active at T). A repeated sector id ⇒ ErrDobleGasto. A PoST block whose `sector` is not of its `productor` ⇒ ErrAutorizacion; if not active ⇒ ErrSectorInactivo.

Wait, "PruebaSector(id, firmante)" — the proof is a transaction? It's listed under Tx variants. Hmm, "AltaSector" and "PruebaSector" are Tx variants. And "sector" is a field of Bloque (PoST). So a PoST block declares `sector` and must contain a PruebaSector? Let me re-read §4.2:

"`sector::Int` (PoST; 0 si `SEC-0`)"

And ORDEN §3.8: "`SEC-A` v0: alta y prueba de sector solo en fase PoW; ... Un sector está activo si su prueba se aplicó en `h_prueba ≤ h_alta + P_sec` y `h_alta + M_sec ≤ h` actual (o cualquier punto de la fase PoST si ya lo estaba en `T`). Una `PruebaSector` aplicada con `h > h_alta + P_sec` ⇒ `ErrPruebaTardia`; un id de sector repetido ⇒ `ErrDobleGasto`; un bloque PoST cuyo `sector` no es de su `productor` ⇒ `ErrAutorizacion`, y si no está activo ⇒ `ErrSectorInactivo`."

Hmm, "alta y prueba de sector solo en fase PoW". So both AltaSector and PruebaSector are only valid in PoW phase! Wait, that seems odd: "prueba" of sector in PoW? Let me re-read. Actually "alta y prueba de sector solo en fase PoW" — hmm, but then how does a PoST block reference a sector? The `sector` field of a PoST block must be active. Its proof was applied during PoW at h_prueba. Then "o cualquier punto de la fase PoST si ya lo estaba en T" means if the sector was already active at the terminal T, it's active throughout PoST.

Hmm, so SEC-A v0: registration and proof both happen in PoW. That's strange but it's what the order says: "alta y prueba de sector solo en fase PoW". Actually wait, let me re-read the ORDEN §3.7: "Fuera de alcance v0 (rechazo explícito ErrFueraDeAlcanceV0): ... altas de sector en fase PoST." So alta in PoST is out of scope. And §3.8 begins "SEC-A v0: alta y prueba de sector solo en fase PoW" — yes, both.

Hmm, but the contract §4 table says "Alta de sector (SEC-A) | sí, desde H_dep | sí". The order overrides PoST to out-of-scope. And the order says "prueba ... solo en fase PoW", overriding contract as well? The contract table doesn't list "PruebaSector" separately. Hmm.

Wait, actually maybe the order means: the abstract sector interface (SEC-A) is only fully exercised in PoW; in PoST it's out of scope. And a PoST block's `sector` field refers to a sector activated during PoW. So `PruebaSector` Tx appears only in PoW blocks. And in PoST, the block just has a `sector` field, checked for active.

Hmm, but then what about "una PruebaSector aplicada con h > h_alta + P_sec ⇒ ErrPruebaTardia". So PruebaSector is a Tx inside a PoW block.

But wait: "PruebaSector(id, firmante)". The proof must be applied at height h_prueba. The sector must belong to firmante? Probably. And "un bloque PoST cuyo sector no es de su productor ⇒ ErrAutorizacion". So sector has an owner P (recorded at alta). For a PruebaSector, presumably firmante == owner, else ErrAutorizacion.

Also "un id de sector repetido ⇒ ErrDobleGasto": a repeated sector id (either in AltaSector or PruebaSector?) Hmm. If AltaSector with an id already registered → ErrDobleGasto. Or PruebaSector twice for the same id → ErrDobleGasto? "un id de sector repetido ⇒ ErrDobleGasto". I'd interpret: high-level, any operation reusing an existing id causes ErrDobleGasto. Let me make AltaSector reject if id exists; PruebaSector reject if the sector already had its proof applied (id already proven) — actually since the sector entry exists from alta, "repeated" for PruebaSector would mean proving twice. Hmm. But also, a sector must be active for a PoST block to use it. The "plazo" is h_alta + P_sec.

Let me design the sector record: `SectorReg = (id, P, alta_en, activa_en, plazo, prueba_en::Union{Nothing,Int})`. `activa` = proof applied at `prueba_en ≤ plazo` and (current height ≥ activa_en). Actually "Un sector está activo si su prueba se aplicó en h_prueba ≤ h_alta + P_sec y h_alta + M_sec ≤ h actual". So both conditions. Once active at T, it stays active through PoST ("o cualquier punto de la fase PoST si ya lo estaba en T").

Hmm, the "activa_en" in contract state is h_alta + M_sec. Yes.

But note: a sector can be "alta" (registered) but not yet proven → not active. Its proof must be applied by plazo = h_alta + P_sec. If not proven by then, the alta "caduca sin activar" (TRN-12). So the sector never becomes active.

Now, "PruebaSector applied with h > h_alta + P_sec ⇒ ErrPruebaTardia". And "producción con sector no activo ⇒ ErrSectorInactivo".

4. **`Φ` (phi).** `phi(E, P)`: Σ activo ≥ S_min, #{claves con activo ≥ q} ≥ K_min, and in SEC-A #{sectores activos} ≥ C_min.

Note: "Σ activo" — sum over all keys of activo. "#{claves con activo ≥ q}" — count keys whose activo ≥ q. In SEC-A, count active sectors ≥ C_min.

Note ordering §3.9(b): promote pending before evaluating Φ at end of block (step e). Actually Φ(Estado(T)) is evaluated when deciding terminal, which is step (e) after applying txs. And step (c) in PoST checks producer guarantee activo ≥ q.

5. **requisito(B) = q constant.** TRN-07 requires garantia_activa(productor, past(B)) ≥ q > 0. And X-10: declared requisito ignored. Also note §4.3 `requisito(past, P)` = constant q.

6. **`seleccionar`.** Function pure: given a set of blocks, validate contextually (a block is valid only if its parent is valid and applying it on the parent's state doesn't error), discard invalid and descendants, then apply SeleccionTransversal.

Wait — this is tricky. `seleccionar(conjunto_de_bloques, P) -> punta`. It must validate the whole set from genesis. Let me think of the algorithm:
- Build map id->bloque. Find genesis (padre=0).
- Compute valid set: genesis valid (if it passes checks); then any block whose parent is valid and `aplicar(estado_padre, B)` doesn't error, is valid.
- Actually this computes a "candidate tree" of valid blocks. Then apply FC-3 etc.

But there's subtlety: `aplicar` needs the state of the parent, which requires applying the parent chain. This is essentially a DFS. Given small sizes (≤ 12 blocks), fine.

Then SeleccionTransversal:
- FC-3 (default): if there exists some valid PoST block, maximize sum of PoST weights of the suffix; tie-break by smaller id of terminal, then smaller id of the tip. If not, maximum accumulated PoW work; tie-break by smaller id of tip.
- FC-1: first max accumulated PoW work of the PoW prefix (up to the terminal, or up to the tip if no terminal); then PoST weight; same tie-breaks.
- FC-2: like FC-3 but with the `Corte` interface evaluated with W_min = 0.

Hmm, this needs care. Let me parse.

The "punta" (tip) is a leaf of the valid block tree? Or a block? The selection returns a punta (tip), and the history selected is the chain from genesis to that tip. Since each block has one parent, the valid set is a tree (forest, but one genesis). So a tip is any valid block with no valid children? Actually, a "punta" could be any valid block, but selection naturally picks a maximal one.

FC-3: "si existe algún bloque PoST válido, máxima suma de pesos PoST del sufijo". The "sufijo" from the terminal to the tip. So for each valid tip that has a terminal in its ancestry, compute the PoST suffix weight = sum of `peso` of PoST blocks on the path from (after terminal) to tip. Wait, the terminal itself is PoW, so suffix = PoST blocks descendant of T to tip. Then maximize. Tie-break menor id del terminal, luego menor id de la punta.

Hmm, "desempate por menor id del terminal y luego menor id de la punta". So among tips with equal max suffix weight, pick the one whose terminal has smallest id; then smallest tip id.

But what about valid PoW tips without PoST suffix when some PoST exists? FC-3 says if there's any valid PoST block, select by max PoST suffix weight. A pure-PoW tip has suffix weight 0 < any positive PoST suffix, so it loses. But what about a PoW tip that is in the PoW phase and also a terminal? Actually if there's a valid PoST block, there's a terminal. The tip could be the terminal itself (PoW) with suffix weight 0. So PoST tips win. Good.

"If not [no valid PoST block], maximum accumulated PoW work; tie-break by smaller id of tip." So among all valid tips, max trabajo_acum; then smaller tip id.

FC-1: "primero máximo trabajo acumulado del prefijo PoW (hasta el terminal, o hasta la punta si no hay terminal); después peso PoST; mismos desempates." So first compare max PoW work of the prefix (the accumulated work up to the terminal if the tip has a terminal, else up to the tip). Then PoST weight. Tie-break terminal id then tip id.

Hmm, "trabajo acumulado del prefijo PoW (hasta el terminal, o hasta la punta si no hay terminal)". So for a tip with terminal T, prefix work = trabajo_acum(T). For a tip without terminal (still PoW), prefix work = trabajo_acum(tip). Then among max, choose by PoST weight (desc). Then tie-break.

FC-2: "como FC-3 pero con la interfaz Corte evaluada con W_min = 0." So terminal determination uses W_min=0 instead of P.W_min. That changes which blocks are terminals (more blocks qualify), hence suffix weights/terminal ids. But the arithmetic accumulation is same.

Hmm, but note: if Corte = CUT-H (only height), W_min is ignored anyway. FC-2 only differs from FC-3 when Corte is HWΦ or W. Hmm, when Corte=H, FC-2==FC-3. When Corte=W, W_min=0 makes more blocks terminals.

Wait, also `Corte ∈ {HWΦ, H, W}`. So the `Corte` interface has three candidates. The order §4.3 `es_terminal(historia_hasta_B, P)` uses the chosen Corte. Let me define:
- CUT-HWΦ (default): altura ≥ H_corte_min AND trabajo_acum ≥ W_min AND Φ.
- CUT-H: only altura ≥ H_corte_min? "CUT-H (solo altura)".
- CUT-W: only trabajo ≥ W_min (type TTD).

